# Shared review rooms

## Architecture

- `src/teams.ts` – Hono sub-app mounted at `/api/teams` (all routes behind the existing `requireAuth`, and the existing
  same-origin check for mutations). Teams, membership and team-upload metadata live in D1 (`migrations/0002_teams.sql`;
  earlier migrations untouched). Every authorization decision reads D1 directly – there is no authorization cache.
- Team files: R2 key is `teams/<team>/<server uuid>`, derived from the verified team in the URL plus the id; the D1 row
  must belong to that same team, so an id from another team is a 404 and cannot read or delete bytes. Bodies are bounded
  while streaming (`readBounded`, shared with personal uploads), so chunked bodies over 1 MiB get 413.
- `src/room.ts` – `ReviewRoom`, a SQLite Durable Object (one per `team:room`), using the hibernation API
  (`acceptWebSocket`, `serializeAttachment`, `webSocketMessage/Close/Error`, `setWebSocketAutoResponse` for `ping`→`pong`).
  No socket map, no timers. Messages go into a SQLite table with a persisted `last_seq` counter (so `seq` keeps rising
  after pruning/restart), are committed in one transaction, then broadcast. History keeps the latest 500; a new
  connection gets the latest 50.
- The Worker route builds a brand-new header set for the DO (`X-Room-*` from the verified session); client headers are
  never forwarded. The DO is only reachable through the `ROOMS` binding; there is no forwarding/admin route.
- Frontend: `public/teams.html` (linked from the home page).

## Commands

`pnpm typecheck`, `pnpm test`, `pnpm build` (dry run), `pnpm db:local` (local D1 migrations), `pnpm dev`.

## Behaviour notes / limitations

- Non-members get 403 on team routes (including unknown team ids). Only `user_alice` / `user_bob` can be added.
- Socket authorization is a lease: min(5 min, session expiry), checked at handshake against D1. After the lease ends,
  the next message from that socket re-checks session + membership in D1; sockets that are idle past their lease are
  closed (code 4401) when the next broadcast happens, and the page reconnects once automatically. So a removed member
  or logged-out session can keep an already-open socket for up to 5 minutes, but cannot open a new one.
- Invalid input (non-JSON, binary, > 4096 bytes, blank or > 1000 code points) gets an `{type:"error"}` event and is not stored.
- Delivery is at-least-once-ish best effort per live socket; there is no replay/dedup protocol, and no exactly-once claim.
- Not covered by automated tests: lease expiry (wall clock based), a Wrangler process restart (history is in DO SQLite
  storage, which `wrangler dev` persists under `.wrangler/state`), and the `teams.html` page in a real browser – the page
  was written against the spec but only the API and WebSocket layers are exercised by tests.
- Membership management is API-only.
